   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m15","m25"]},"explication":"**bored** et **surprised** : l’adjectif en **-ed** dit ce que ressent la personne."},{"cle":{"type":"categories","items":{"i0":"c1","i1":"c0","i2":"c0","i3":"c1","i4":"c1","i5":"c0"}},"explication":"**-ed** pour la personne qui ressent, **-ing** pour la chose qui provoque."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["interested"]]},"affichage":"interested","explication":"**interested** : Tom ressent l’intérêt, donc **-ed**."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["d","a","b","e","c"]},"explication":"**a big old wooden table** : taille → âge → matière."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('5f00280a-ce05-524a-94b3-8eb4ed4305a2'::uuid, 3, 3, 15, 50, 5,
   $ex${"titre":"Un appartement à louer","competence":"argumenter","situation":"Ella cherche un logement pour ses études. Elle lit une annonce, puis raconte sa visite à une amie.","documents":[{"id":"annonce","type":"texte","genre":"article","titre":"To let","blocs":["To let: a lovely small flat in Leeds. Two bedrooms, a big kitchen.","Only a ten-minute walk from the station. Perfect for the young and for families."],"source":"Annonce écrite pour l’exercice"},{"id":"visite","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Messages d’Ella et Jo","participants":[{"nom":"Ella","emoji":"👧","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Jo","emoji":"👩","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Ella","texte":"The visit was long and tiring."},{"qui":"Jo","texte":"Poor you! Was the flat nice?"}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche l’**adjectif composé** de l’annonce.","aide":"Il contient un trait d’union et un nombre.","type":"zone","document":"annonce","multiple":false},{"enonce":"La gare est à cinq minutes. Complète l’expression.","aide":"Dans un adjectif composé, le nom reste au singulier.","type":"trous","segments":["It’s a ",0," walk."],"banque":["five-minutes","fives-minute","five minute","five-minute"]},{"enonce":"Quelle phrase est correcte ?","aide":"*The young* désigne tout un groupe de personnes.","type":"choix","options":[{"id":"a","texte":"The young needs cheap flats."},{"id":"b","texte":"The young need cheap flats."},{"id":"c","texte":"The young peoples need cheap flats."},{"id":"d","texte":"The youngs need cheap flats."}],"multiple":false},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre : « une belle grande vieille maison ». La phrase commence par a.","aide":"Opinion, puis taille, puis âge.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"old"},{"id":"b","texte":"nice"},{"id":"c","texte":"house"},{"id":"d","texte":"a"},{"id":"e","texte":"big"}]},{"enonce":"Touche l’adjectif qui dit ce qui **provoque** la fatigue d’Ella.","aide":"La visite provoque la fatigue.","type":"zone","document":"visite","multiple":false}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m15"]},"explication":"**ten-minute** : adjectif composé, avec un trait d’union et le nom au **singulier**."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["five minute"]]},"affichage":"five-minute","explication":"**a five-minute walk** : trait d’union et nom au singulier."},{"cle":{"type":"choix","ids":["b"]},"explication":"**The young need…** : un adjectif substantivé désigne un groupe et se construit avec un verbe au **pluriel**."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["d","b","e","a","c"]},"explication":"**a nice big old house** : opinion → taille → âge."},{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m5"]},"explication":"**tiring** : l’adjectif en **-ing** décrit ce qui provoque le sentiment (la visite)."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('8c4e15fc-abcd-5587-9bfe-829e9d2f3b87'::uuid, 1, 1, 5, 20, 3,
   $ex${"titre":"Une invitation à la pizzeria","competence":"comprendre-langue","situation":"Lily invite Tom à dîner à la pizzeria. Elle lui pose plusieurs questions par message.","documents":[{"id":"pizza","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Messages de Lily et Tom","participants":[{"nom":"Lily","emoji":"👧","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Tom","emoji":"👦","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Lily","texte":"Do you like pizza? Are you free tonight? Can you come at six?"},{"qui":"Tom","texte":"Yes, I do! I’m free, and I can come at six."}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche les **trois auxiliaires** qui ouvrent les questions de Lily.","aide":"Dans une question, l’auxiliaire se place devant le sujet *you*.","type":"zone","document":"pizza","multiple":true},{"enonce":"Dans la réponse « Yes, I **do** », à quoi sert *do* ?","aide":"Tom ne répète pas « like pizza ».","type":"choix","options":[{"id":"a","texte":"À former un temps composé, comme dans « I have seen »"},{"id":"b","texte":"À poser une question, comme dans « Do you like it? »"},{"id":"c","texte":"À éviter de répéter le verbe like : c’est une reprise courte"},{"id":"d","texte":"À former une négation, comme dans « I don’t know »"}],"multiple":false},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre : « Peux-tu venir à six heures ? » La phrase commence par Can.","aide":"L’auxiliaire se place devant le sujet.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"Can"},{"id":"b","texte":"at"},{"id":"c","texte":"come"},{"id":"d","texte":"you"},{"id":"e","texte":"six"}]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m0","m4","m8"]},"explication":"**Do, Are, Can** : l’auxiliaire passe devant le sujet pour former la question."},{"cle":{"type":"choix","ids":["c"]},"explication":"**Yes, I do.** : l’auxiliaire permet la **reprise courte**, sans répéter le verbe lexical."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["a","d","c","b","e"]},"explication":"**Can you come at six?** Auxiliaire, sujet, verbe lexical."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('8c4e15fc-abcd-5587-9bfe-829e9d2f3b87'::uuid, 2, 2, 10, 35, 4,
   $ex${"titre":"Au club de sport","competence":"manier-langue","situation":"Jack et Nina se retrouvent au club de sport. Ils parlent de la sœur de Nina.","documents":[{"id":"club","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Messages de Jack et Nina","participants":[{"nom":"Jack","emoji":"👦","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Nina","emoji":"👧","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Jack","texte":"Does your sister play tennis?"},{"qui":"Nina","texte":"No, she doesn’t. But she can swim, and she is learning to dive."}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche les **quatre auxiliaires** de la conversation.","aide":"Les autres verbes (play, swim, learning) portent le sens.","type":"zone","document":"club","multiple":true},{"enonce":"Range chaque verbe : porte-t-il un **sens** (lexical) ou aide-t-il à **construire** la phrase (auxiliaire) ?","aide":"Un auxiliaire n’a pas de sens propre : il sert à former une question, une négation, un temps.","type":"categories","categories":[{"id":"c0","texte":"Verbe lexical"},{"id":"c1","texte":"Auxiliaire"}],"items":[{"id":"i0","texte":"run"},{"id":"i1","texte":"think"},{"id":"i2","texte":"can"},{"id":"i3","texte":"must"},{"id":"i4","texte":"believe"},{"id":"i5","texte":"eat"},{"id":"i6","texte":"will"}]},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre la négation de « He plays tennis ». La phrase commence par He.","aide":"La négation se construit avec do au présent, et le verbe revient à la base.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"doesn’t"},{"id":"b","texte":"He"},{"id":"c","texte":"play"},{"id":"d","texte":"tennis"}]},{"enonce":"Associe chaque auxiliaire à ce qu’il forme.","aide":"Chaque famille d’auxiliaires a sa fonction.","type":"association","gauche":[{"id":"g0","texte":"be"},{"id":"g1","texte":"have"},{"id":"g2","texte":"do"},{"id":"g3","texte":"can"}],"droite":[{"id":"d0","texte":"les formes en -ING et le passif"},{"id":"d1","texte":"la capacité (un modal)"},{"id":"d2","texte":"la négation et la question aux temps simples"},{"id":"d3","texte":"les temps composés"}]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m0","m7","m10","m14"]},"explication":"**Does, doesn’t, can, is** sont des auxiliaires : ils forment la question, la négation ou les temps."},{"cle":{"type":"categories","items":{"i0":"c0","i1":"c0","i2":"c1","i3":"c1","i4":"c0","i5":"c0","i6":"c1"}},"explication":"**eat, think, believe, run** portent un sens ; **can, must, will** sont des modaux, donc des auxiliaires."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["b","a","c","d"]},"explication":"**He doesn’t play tennis.** *Doesn’t* porte la marque de la 3e personne : play n’a plus de -s."},{"cle":{"type":"association","paires":{"g0":"d0","g3":"d1","g2":"d2","g1":"d3"}},"explication":"**be** → -ING et passif ; **have** → temps composés ; **do** → négation et question aux temps simples ; **can** est un modal."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('8c4e15fc-abcd-5587-9bfe-829e9d2f3b87'::uuid, 3, 3, 15, 50, 5,
   $ex${"titre":"Les devoirs de Dan","competence":"analyser-document","situation":"Maya demande à Dan s’il a fini ses devoirs, puis elle l’invite au parc. Elle note ensuite les questions à poser dans un tableau.","documents":[{"id":"devoirs","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Messages de Maya et Dan","participants":[{"nom":"Maya","emoji":"👧","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Dan","emoji":"👦","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Maya","texte":"Dan, have you finished your homework?"},{"qui":"Dan","texte":"No, I haven’t. I have a lot of maths to do. And you?"},{"qui":"Maya","texte":"Yes, I have. Can you come to the park?"},{"qui":"Dan","texte":"I can’t. I don’t have my bike."}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"},{"id":"questions","type":"tableau","titre":"Les questions de Maya","colonnes":["Affirmation","Question"],"lignes":[["You are ready.","Are you ready?"],["You have a bike.","?"],["You have finished.","?"]],"source":"Tableau écrit pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Dans le message de Dan, touche le « have » qui est un **verbe lexical** (il veut dire « avoir à faire »).","aide":"Un verbe lexical porte un sens ; ici, ce n’est pas un temps composé.","type":"zone","document":"devoirs","multiple":false},{"enonce":"Complète les deux questions du tableau.","aide":"Have lexical demande do ; have auxiliaire passe devant le sujet.","type":"trous","segments":["Question 2: ",0," you have a bike? Question 3: ",1," you finished?"],"banque":["Can","Have","Are","Do"]},{"enonce":"Dan dit : « I don’t have my bike. » Pourquoi « don’t » et pas « haven’t » ?","aide":"Souviens-toi : have lexical se nie avec do.","type":"choix","options":[{"id":"a","texte":"Parce que have est ici un verbe lexical : il lui faut do"},{"id":"b","texte":"Parce que don’t est la forme de la 3e personne"},{"id":"c","texte":"Parce que haven’t est une forme trop familière en anglais"},{"id":"d","texte":"Parce que bike est un nom indénombrable en anglais"}],"multiple":false},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre la question de Maya. La phrase commence par Have.","aide":"L’auxiliaire have passe devant le sujet.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"finished"},{"id":"b","texte":"homework"},{"id":"c","texte":"your"},{"id":"d","texte":"Have"},{"id":"e","texte":"you"}]},{"enonce":"Dan ne peut pas venir. Complète sa réponse courte.","aide":"On reprend l’auxiliaire de la question.","type":"trous","segments":["Can you come? — No, I ",0,"."],"banque":["isn’t","haven’t","don’t","can’t"]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m10"]},"explication":"**I have a lot of maths to do** : *have* est lexical (il exprime l’idée d’avoir quelque chose), alors que dans *Have you finished?* il est auxiliaire."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["do"],["have"]]},"affichage":"Do · Have","explication":"**Do you have a bike?** (have lexical → do) et **Have you finished?** (have auxiliaire → il passe devant le sujet)."},{"cle":{"type":"choix","ids":["a"]},"explication":"**Have lexical (posséder)** se construit avec **do** : *I don’t have…*."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["d","e","a","c","b"]},"explication":"**Have you finished your homework?** Auxiliaire, sujet, participe passé."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["can t"]]},"affichage":"can’t","explication":"**No, I can’t.** La reprise courte reprend l’auxiliaire de la question (ici can)."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('e1a5cb5f-1474-5c26-946f-122bbe083379'::uuid, 1, 1, 5, 20, 3,
   $ex${"titre":"Leo se présente","competence":"comprendre-langue","situation":"Leo cherche un correspondant. Il écrit un petit message de présentation.","documents":[{"id":"profil","type":"texte","genre":"lettre","titre":"Hello!","blocs":["Hi! My name is Leo and I am 12 years old. I’m English.","I have a sister. My sister is nine. She is a very good swimmer."],"source":"Message écrit pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche le verbe que Leo emploie pour dire **son âge**.","aide":"Le français dit « j’ai », pas l’anglais.","type":"zone","document":"profil","multiple":false},{"enonce":"Complète avec le verbe qui convient.","aide":"Pour l’âge, on emploie be.","type":"trous","segments":["My sister ",0," nine."],"banque":["have","is","are","has"]},{"enonce":"Comment dit-on « J’ai soif » ?","aide":"Comme pour l’âge, l’anglais emploie be.","type":"choix","options":[{"id":"a","texte":"I do thirsty."},{"id":"b","texte":"I have a thirst."},{"id":"c","texte":"I am thirsty."},{"id":"d","texte":"I have thirsty."}],"multiple":false}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m7"]},"explication":"**I am 12** : pour l’âge, l’anglais emploie **be**, jamais *have*."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["is"]]},"affichage":"is","explication":"**My sister is nine.** Age = verbe **be**."},{"cle":{"type":"choix","ids":["c"]},"explication":"**I am thirsty.** *Be* s’emploie là où le français dit « avoir » : l’âge, la faim, la soif, le froid…"}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('e1a5cb5f-1474-5c26-946f-122bbe083379'::uuid, 2, 2, 10, 35, 4,
   $ex${"titre":"Une carte postale du zoo","competence":"manier-langue","situation":"Max et sa famille ont passé la journée au zoo. Sa sœur envoie une carte postale à sa grand-mère.","documents":[{"id":"carte","type":"texte","genre":"lettre","titre":"Postcard","blocs":["Dear Gran,","We were at the zoo yesterday. It was sunny. Max was hungry, so he had two sandwiches.","I had an ice cream and my parents had a coffee. Love, Ruby"],"source":"Carte écrite pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche les **trois formes de be au prétérit** de la carte.","aide":"Elles commencent par w-.","type":"zone","document":"carte","multiple":true},{"enonce":"Range chaque forme selon le verbe dont elle vient.","aide":"Be : was, were, been. Have : have, has, had.","type":"categories","categories":[{"id":"c0","texte":"Forme de be"},{"id":"c1","texte":"Forme de have"}],"items":[{"id":"i0","texte":"had"},{"id":"i1","texte":"were"},{"id":"i2","texte":"been"},{"id":"i3","texte":"has"},{"id":"i4","texte":"have"},{"id":"i5","texte":"was"}]},{"enonce":"Associe chaque phrase française à sa traduction.","aide":"L’anglais emploie be là où le français dit « avoir ».","type":"association","gauche":[{"id":"g0","texte":"J’ai chaud."},{"id":"g1","texte":"Il a sept ans."},{"id":"g2","texte":"Nous avons soif."},{"id":"g3","texte":"Elles ont raison."}],"droite":[{"id":"d0","texte":"He is seven."},{"id":"d1","texte":"We are thirsty."},{"id":"d2","texte":"They are right."},{"id":"d3","texte":"I am hot."}]},{"enonce":"Comment interroge-t-on « She has breakfast at eight » (have d’action) ?","aide":"Have d’action se construit avec do.","type":"choix","options":[{"id":"a","texte":"Does she have breakfast at eight?"},{"id":"b","texte":"Has she got breakfast at eight?"},{"id":"c","texte":"Have she breakfast at eight?"},{"id":"d","texte":"Is she have breakfast at eight?"}],"multiple":false}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m3","m9","m12"]},"explication":"**were, was, was** : prétérit de **be** (was avec I, he, she, it ; were avec you, we, they)."},{"cle":{"type":"categories","items":{"i0":"c1","i1":"c0","i2":"c0","i3":"c1","i4":"c1","i5":"c0"}},"explication":"**was, were, been** viennent de be ; **has, had, have** viennent de have."},{"cle":{"type":"association","paires":{"g1":"d0","g2":"d1","g3":"d2","g0":"d3"}},"explication":"**be** pour la chaleur, l’âge, la soif, avoir raison : on ne dit pas *have* dans ces cas."},{"cle":{"type":"choix","ids":["a"]},"explication":"**Does she have breakfast at eight?** *Have breakfast* est une action : elle demande **do**."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('e1a5cb5f-1474-5c26-946f-122bbe083379'::uuid, 3, 3, 15, 50, 5,
   $ex${"titre":"Des contractions à deviner","competence":"argumenter","situation":"Tom et Anna parlent de leur ami Ben. Les contractions « ’s » ne veulent pas toujours dire la même chose. Un tableau rappelle le prétérit de be et de have.","documents":[{"id":"ben","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Messages de Tom et Anna","participants":[{"nom":"Tom","emoji":"👦","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Anna","emoji":"👧","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Tom","texte":"Is Ben at home?"},{"qui":"Anna","texte":"No, he’s gone to the shop. He’s hungry."}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"},{"id":"prete","type":"tableau","titre":"Be et have au prétérit","colonnes":["Sujet","be","have"],"lignes":[["I","was","had"],["you","?","had"],["she","was","?"]],"source":"Tableau écrit pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Range chaque phrase selon ce que représente la contraction ’s.","aide":"C’est ce qui suit qui tranche : un adjectif, un verbe en -ing ou un nom → is ; un participe passé → has.","type":"categories","categories":[{"id":"c0","texte":"’s = is"},{"id":"c1","texte":"’s = has"}],"items":[{"id":"i0","texte":"It’s been a long day."},{"id":"i1","texte":"She’s got a new bike."},{"id":"i2","texte":"He’s finished his homework."},{"id":"i3","texte":"She’s a teacher."},{"id":"i4","texte":"He’s tired."},{"id":"i5","texte":"It’s raining."}]},{"enonce":"Touche la contraction où **’s veut dire has**.","aide":"Regarde ce qui suit : gone est un participe passé.","type":"zone","document":"ben","multiple":false},{"enonce":"D’après le tableau, complète le prétérit.","aide":"Be au prétérit : was ou were ; have au prétérit : une seule forme.","type":"trous","segments":["You ",0," (be) at home. She ",1," (have) a bike."],"banque":["has","was","had","were"]},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre : « Nous étions au parc hier. » La phrase commence par We.","aide":"We prend were au prétérit.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"at"},{"id":"b","texte":"were"},{"id":"c","texte":"yesterday"},{"id":"d","texte":"park"},{"id":"e","texte":"We"},{"id":"f","texte":"the"}]},{"enonce":"Complète la question avec have got.","aide":"Avec have got, on n’emploie pas do.","type":"trous","segments":[0," you got a bike?"],"banque":["Do","Are","Does","Have"]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"categories","items":{"i0":"c1","i1":"c1","i2":"c1","i3":"c0","i4":"c0","i5":"c0"}},"explication":"**is** devant un adjectif, un nom ou -ing ; **has** devant un participe passé (finished, got, been)."},{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m5"]},"explication":"**he’s gone** = he **has** gone (temps composé). *He’s hungry* = he **is** hungry."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["were"],["had"]]},"affichage":"were · had","explication":"**you were** (be au prétérit avec you) et **she had** (had pour toutes les personnes)."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["e","b","a","f","d","c"]},"explication":"**We were at the park yesterday.** Be au prétérit avec we : were."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["have"]]},"affichage":"Have","explication":"**Have you got a bike?** *Have got* se construit **sans do** (britannique)."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('8a8605d5-ea6f-5b68-92e4-da445fea8267'::uuid, 1, 1, 5, 20, 3,
   $ex${"titre":"Eva et Sam parlent de sport","competence":"comprendre-langue","situation":"Eva interroge Sam sur son frère. Tous les deux parlent de sport.","documents":[{"id":"sport","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Messages d’Eva et Sam","participants":[{"nom":"Eva","emoji":"👧","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Sam","emoji":"👦","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Eva","texte":"Does your brother play football?"},{"qui":"Sam","texte":"No, he doesn’t. He plays tennis."},{"qui":"Eva","texte":"Do you play tennis too?"},{"qui":"Sam","texte":"Yes, I do."}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche les **deux auxiliaires** qui ouvrent les questions d’Eva.","aide":"Ils sont en début de phrase, avant le sujet.","type":"zone","document":"sport","multiple":true},{"enonce":"Touche le mot qui exprime la **négation** dans la réponse de Sam.","aide":"Il se termine par -n’t.","type":"zone","document":"sport","multiple":false},{"enonce":"Complète la phrase de Sam avec la bonne forme du verbe.","aide":"Après doesn’t, le verbe revient à sa base.","type":"trous","segments":["He doesn’t ",0," football."],"banque":["plays","play","playing","played"]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m0","m11"]},"explication":"**Does** (he, she, it) et **Do** (I, you, we, they) servent à poser la question au présent."},{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m7"]},"explication":"**doesn’t** = does + not : négation à la 3e personne du singulier."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["play"]]},"affichage":"play","explication":"**He doesn’t play football.** *Doesn’t* porte déjà la marque de la 3e personne."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('8a8605d5-ea6f-5b68-92e4-da445fea8267'::uuid, 2, 2, 10, 35, 4,
   $ex${"titre":"Les erreurs du cahier","competence":"manier-langue","situation":"Une élève a recopié quatre phrases dans son cahier, mais trois contiennent une erreur de verbe. Aide-la à les corriger.","documents":[{"id":"cahier","type":"texte","genre":"consigne","titre":"Le cahier d’Inès","blocs":["1. She doesn’t works here.","2. Did you went to the cinema?","3. He didn’t played football.","4. What do you do?"],"source":"Phrases écrites pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche les **trois verbes fautifs**.","aide":"Après doesn’t, didn’t et did, le verbe doit être à la base.","type":"zone","document":"cahier","multiple":true},{"enonce":"Complète la phrase au passé.","aide":"La phrase est au passé, et le verbe go est à la base.","type":"trous","segments":["He ",0," go to school yesterday."],"banque":["doesn’t","didn’t","don’t","wasn’t"]},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre : « Qu’est-ce qu’elle fait dans la vie ? » La phrase commence par What.","aide":"Le premier do est l’auxiliaire, le second est le verbe.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"do"},{"id":"b","texte":"What"},{"id":"c","texte":"does"},{"id":"d","texte":"she"}]},{"enonce":"Range chaque question : demande-t-elle l’auxiliaire **do** ?","aide":"Pas de do avec be, avec un modal ou quand who est le sujet.","type":"categories","categories":[{"id":"c0","texte":"Avec do"},{"id":"c1","texte":"Sans do"}],"items":[{"id":"i0","texte":"Is she a teacher?"},{"id":"i1","texte":"Can you swim?"},{"id":"i2","texte":"What do you do?"},{"id":"i3","texte":"Does she work here?"},{"id":"i4","texte":"Who broke the window?"},{"id":"i5","texte":"Did you go to school?"}]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m3","m8","m15"]},"explication":"**works, went, played** : do porte déjà la marque du temps et de la personne, le verbe reprend sa **base**."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["didn t"]]},"affichage":"didn’t","explication":"**He didn’t go** : une seule marque de temps, portée par **didn’t**."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["b","c","d","a"]},"explication":"**What does she do?** Auxiliaire *does*, sujet, verbe lexical *do*."},{"cle":{"type":"categories","items":{"i0":"c1","i1":"c1","i2":"c0","i3":"c0","i4":"c1","i5":"c0"}},"explication":"**Do** avec les verbes lexicaux ; **pas de do** avec be, can, ni quand *who* est sujet."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('8a8605d5-ea6f-5b68-92e4-da445fea8267'::uuid, 3, 3, 15, 50, 5,
   $ex${"titre":"Après le concert","competence":"argumenter","situation":"Nora interroge Ben sur le concert d’hier. Un tableau résume l’enquête menée dans la classe.","documents":[{"id":"concert","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Messages de Nora et Ben","participants":[{"nom":"Nora","emoji":"👧","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Ben","emoji":"👦","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Nora","texte":"Did you enjoy the concert?"},{"qui":"Ben","texte":"I did enjoy it! It was amazing."},{"qui":"Nora","texte":"Who bought the tickets?"},{"qui":"Ben","texte":"Mia did. She doesn’t like crowds, but she did come."}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"},{"id":"enquete","type":"tableau","titre":"Concert survey","colonnes":["Question","Yes","No"],"lignes":[["Did you enjoy it?","18","2"],["Did you go by bus?","7","13"]],"source":"Chiffres inventés pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche les **deux fois** où *did* sert à **insister** (il est suivi d’un verbe).","aide":"Pas dans la question ni dans la reprise « Mia did. ».","type":"zone","document":"concert","multiple":true},{"enonce":"Pourquoi écrit-on « Who **bought** the tickets? » sans did ?","aide":"Pense à ce qui fait l’action.","type":"choix","options":[{"id":"a","texte":"Parce que who est le sujet de la question"},{"id":"b","texte":"Parce que tickets est au pluriel"},{"id":"c","texte":"Parce que bought est un verbe irrégulier"},{"id":"d","texte":"Parce que la question est au présent"}],"multiple":false},{"enonce":"Mia ne supporte pas la foule (présent). Complète.","aide":"Mia est à la 3e personne du singulier, au présent.","type":"trous","segments":["Mia ",0," like crowds."],"banque":["don’t","isn’t","didn’t","doesn’t"]},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre la question de Nora. La phrase commence par Did.","aide":"Auxiliaire, sujet, verbe à la base.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"the"},{"id":"b","texte":"concert"},{"id":"c","texte":"you"},{"id":"d","texte":"enjoy"},{"id":"e","texte":"Did"}]},{"enonce":"D’a